<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Perimeter and Semiperimeter of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />. The perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=12\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. The semiperimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. This is exactly what the Right Triangle Perimeter and Semiperimeter Calculator returns for these three inputs.</p>
  <p>The perimeter describes the complete boundary of the triangle: the total length you would trace if you walked all three sides and returned to the start. The semiperimeter is half of that numerical total. Both are lengths, measured in units like metres, centimetres, feet, or inches. Neither one is an area, and neither one is reported in square units.</p>
  <p>Three side measurements are required because the boundary has three edges, and the hypotenuse must be included: it is one of the three edges even though it never touches the right angle. The semiperimeter is half the perimeter because several advanced formulas, including Heron&apos;s formula and the inradius relation, are built around half the boundary rather than the whole. These two calculations are useful whenever a triangular outline needs measuring: edging a garden, trimming a frame, or feeding a length into the next formula in a longer problem.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Right triangle geometry -->
<section id="sec-right-triangle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Before any addition starts, the three sides must be labelled without error. The symbols below stay fixed through the whole guide, and the calculator uses the same names.</p>

  <h3>Definition of a Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>A right triangle is a three-sided polygon containing exactly one interior angle of 90 degrees. It has three vertices, three sides, two acute angles, and one right angle usually marked with a small square. The two sides forming the right angle are the legs; the side opposite is the hypotenuse. Since the angles of any triangle add to 180 degrees, <span set:html={rich('[[A+B+C=180^\\circ]]')} />, and with <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} /> it follows that <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A+B=90^\\circ}]]]')} />: the two acute angles are complementary, so knowing one determines the other.</p>

  <h3>What Are the Two Legs?</h3>
  <p>Leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> sits opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />; leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> sits opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. The two legs meet at the right-angle vertex and are perpendicular to each other: each one stands at 90 degrees to the other. They form the square corner of the triangle, the kind of edge you would measure along a wall corner or a table edge. For perimeter work their role is simple: both are boundary edges, so both join the sum.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, always the longest side of the triangle. It never forms the right angle; it stretches across from it, connecting the far ends of the two legs. It differs from the legs in position and in length: the legs meet at 90 degrees, while the hypotenuse meets each leg at an acute angle. It must be included when calculating the complete perimeter, because the boundary is not complete until all three edges are counted. If both legs are known, the Pythagorean theorem recovers it through <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}]]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>Why Is the Right Angle Important?</h3>
  <p>The right angle is what makes the triangle a right triangle, and everything else follows from it. It identifies the shape in a drawing or on site: find the square corner and the side labelling falls into place. It is the condition the Pythagorean theorem needs before it can be applied. In construction measurements and engineering drawings, the right angle is the reference everything is squared against: walls, frames, and brackets are checked with it. For verification, the right angle is the promise the three side lengths must keep through <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>How Can You Identify the Correct Side Labels?</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the legs, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse, and in a nondegenerate right triangle <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the longest side. The right angle sits opposite <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, so the vertex carrying the square marker tells you which side is <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />: it is the one that does not touch that vertex. A quick ordering check helps: sort the three numbers, and the largest must play the role of <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. If the largest side is not opposite the square corner, the labels are wrong, and every calculation built on them will be wrong too.</p>

