<!-- Section 3: Perimeter -->
<section id="sec-perimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Perimeter of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The perimeter is the total distance around the outer boundary of a two-dimensional shape. For a triangle it is the sum of its three side lengths, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=a+b+c}]]]')} />, and every side must be included because every side is part of the boundary. Leave one out and the result is not a perimeter; it is a partial walk.</p>

  <h3>Why Does the Hypotenuse Count Toward the Perimeter?</h3>
  <p>The perimeter measures the complete outer boundary, not just the two perpendicular sides. Even though the hypotenuse sits opposite the right angle, it remains one of the three boundary edges: walk the triangle and you walk the hypotenuse too. Leaving it out measures two sides of a broken path, not the outline of a closed triangle. The formula has three terms because the shape has three edges; no edge is optional, however the triangle is oriented on the page or on the ground.</p>

  <h3>How to Calculate Perimeter Step by Step</h3>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />. Substitute into the formula: <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5]]')} />. Add the first two lengths: <span set:html={rich('[[P=7+5]]')} />. Final result: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=12\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Physically, 12 units is the length of a tape laid along all three edges end to end: the full outline of the triangle expressed as one continuous length.</p>

  <h3>What Does Perimeter Tell You in Practical Work?</h3>
  <p>It answers one question in many trades: how much material does the outline consume? That covers border materials, window trim, fence edging, protective strips, decorative framing, cable routes, fabric borders, and metal edge profiles. In each case the perimeter is the number taken to the supplier. It describes the length of the triangle&apos;s complete outline, not the amount of surface inside it: 12 metres of trim says nothing about the area the window covers, and it was never meant to.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Semiperimeter -->
<section id="sec-semiperimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Semiperimeter of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The semiperimeter is exactly half the triangle&apos;s full perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{P}{2}}]]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} />, it follows that <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}}]]]')} />. The addition comes before the division because the semiperimeter halves the whole boundary, not each side separately. Dividing each side first and then adding would give the same number here, but the formula states the correct order of ideas: sum the boundary, then halve it.</p>

  <h3>How to Calculate Semiperimeter Step by Step</h3>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />. Add the three sides: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}]]')} />. Simplify the total: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{12}{2}]]')} />. Final result: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. The semiperimeter is a derived geometric measurement used in several important triangle formulas; it rarely describes something you would measure directly with a tape, which is why the calculator computes it for you.</p>

  <h3>Why Is Semiperimeter Used in Geometry?</h3>
  <p>It is the natural variable of Heron&apos;s formula, of the inradius relation <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} />, and of incircle geometry in general. Triangle area formulas keep producing halves, and the semiperimeter absorbs them all at once, which is why proofs and advanced formulas prefer it over the full perimeter. When a formula asks for <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />, entering <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> instead doubles or otherwise distorts the result, so the distinction is worth keeping sharp from the start.</p>

  <h3>Is Semiperimeter Half the Boundary Physically?</h3>
  <p>Not necessarily. The semiperimeter is half the numerical perimeter: 6 units when the perimeter is 12. It does not necessarily describe a continuous physical path covering half the triangle&apos;s boundary. There is no single walk along the edges that is &quot;the semiperimeter&quot; the way the perimeter is the full walk: the half is a number, not a route. Treating <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> as a physical half-loop leads to confusion; treat it as half the total, the value the formulas need.</p>

  <h3>Can Semiperimeter Be Smaller Than the Longest Side?</h3>
  <p>No. The triangle inequality requires <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]]')} />: the two shorter sides together must exceed the longest side, or the triangle cannot close. Adding <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> to both sides gives <span set:html={rich('[[a+b+c>2c]]')} />, and halving gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s>c}]]]')} />. For a valid nondegenerate triangle, the semiperimeter is greater than the longest side. This is a useful mathematical check: if a reported semiperimeter is smaller than the hypotenuse, the measurements cannot form a real triangle.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: Perimeter vs semiperimeter -->
<section id="sec-difference" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Perimeter vs. Semiperimeter: What Is the Difference?</h2>
  <p>Perimeter: the total length around the triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} />. Semiperimeter: half the total perimeter, <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />. Same three sides, different geometric quantities: one is the whole, the other is the half. They are related by <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{P}{2}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[P=2s]]')} />, and the calculator never uses the two symbols interchangeably.</p>

  <h3>Numerical Comparison</h3>
  <p>Use a 6-8-10 right triangle. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=6+8+10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=24}]]]')} />. Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{24}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=12}]]]')} />. The two numbers differ by a factor of two, always: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{P}{2}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[P=2s]]')} />. The difference is not a different shape or a different triangle; it is the same boundary counted once versus counted as a half.</p>

  <h3>Which Measurement Should You Use?</h3>
  <p>Use the perimeter for bordering, framing, edging, fencing, and material estimates around an object: any job where the question is how much material the outline consumes. Use the semiperimeter for Heron&apos;s formula, inradius relationships, incircle calculations, and geometry problems involving triangle side lengths: any job where a formula asks for half the boundary as its input. The choice is driven by the task, not by preference: a trim estimate wants <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} />, an area proof wants <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>Does Semiperimeter Measure Half the Triangle&apos;s Area?</h3>
  <p>No. Both perimeter and semiperimeter are linear measurements: lengths, reported in metres or feet, never square metres. Area measures the two-dimensional surface enclosed by the triangle and uses square units. Halving a length still gives a length. The warning is worth repeating because the names sound related: the semiperimeter is half the perimeter, not half the area, and no division of a boundary can produce a surface.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('semiperimeter-half.svg')} alt="6-8-10 triangle: perimeter bar of 24 units halved to a semiperimeter bar of 12 units" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">P = 24 halved to s = 12: the full boundary, then half of it.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: The two calculator modes -->
<section id="sec-modes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Two Calculator Modes</h2>
  <p>The calculator keeps the two calculations in two separate modes because they answer different questions from the same three inputs. Choosing the mode is choosing which question is being asked.</p>

  <h3>Perimeter Calculator Mode</h3>
  <p>This mode calculates the total boundary length. Inputs: leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} />. Output: perimeter <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} />. The result is useful for estimating the total length of material required around a triangular outline: the number you take to the supplier when the job is edging, trim, or framing.</p>

  <h3>Semiperimeter Calculator Mode</h3>
  <p>This mode calculates half the total perimeter. Inputs: leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />. Output: semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />. The result is useful as an intermediate value in advanced triangle calculations: the number you feed into Heron&apos;s formula or the inradius relation when the problem continues past the boundary.</p>

  <h3>Why Are There Two Separate Modes?</h3>
  <p>Both calculations use the same three side measurements but return different geometric quantities, and mixing them up is one of the most common errors in this topic. Two labelled modes keep the intent explicit: the button says Calculate Perimeter or Calculate Semiperimeter, and the result is labelled <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> or <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />. The two modes save users from performing manual calculations while keeping the two answers impossible to confuse. Do not describe the semiperimeter mode as an area calculator: its output is a length, and it stays a length.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Perimeter and Semiperimeter Calculator</h2>
  <p>The steps below follow the on-screen layout for each mode, from identifying the measurements to a checked result. The two modes share the same three inputs and differ only in the final calculation.</p>

  <h3>Perimeter Mode</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Select Perimeter mode.</strong> This tells the calculator the goal is the total boundary length.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify leg a.</strong> This is one of the two sides forming the right angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify leg b.</strong> This is the other side forming the right angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the hypotenuse c.</strong> This is the longest side, opposite the right angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter leg a.</strong> Type the measured length into the first field.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter leg b.</strong> Type the measured length into the second field.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the hypotenuse c.</strong> Type the measured length into the third field.</li>
    <li><strong>Select the appropriate units.</strong> All three sides must share one unit before the addition means anything.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Perimeter.</strong> The calculator adds the three sides.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the result.</strong> Confirm that the perimeter exceeds the longest side.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the displayed solution steps.</strong> They show <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} /> with the entered values filled in.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify that the output uses a linear measurement unit.</strong> Metres, centimetres, feet, or inches: never a square unit.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Semiperimeter Mode</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Select Semiperimeter mode.</strong> This tells the calculator the goal is half the boundary.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter leg a.</strong> Same identification as in perimeter mode.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter leg b.</strong> The second side forming the right angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the hypotenuse c.</strong> The longest side, opposite the right angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible measurement units.</strong> One unit for all three sides.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Semiperimeter.</strong> The calculator adds the three sides and halves the total.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the result.</strong> It should be clearly smaller than the perimeter.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify that the result is exactly half the perimeter.</strong> Check <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{P}{2}]]')} /> against the perimeter mode output.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the solution steps and the live visualization.</strong> The steps show the addition and the halving; the drawing shows the triangle those numbers came from.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset when starting a new calculation.</strong> It clears all three fields for a clean start.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The live visualization draws the triangle from the entered sides, which helps catch a mislabelled hypotenuse before the numbers are trusted. The formula diagram shows where each side sits in <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} /> or <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />. Neither mode accepts two sides: the calculator needs all three boundary lengths, so a missing side must be found first, as the missing-side section explains.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: Four foundational examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Right Triangle Perimeter and Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>Four well-known Pythagorean triples show the full calculation for both measurements. Each one adds the three sides for the perimeter, then halves the total for the semiperimeter. The arithmetic is deliberately shown in full so the pattern is unmistakable.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />. Calculate the perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=12\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Calculate the semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Both results are correct because the perimeter counts every boundary edge once, and the semiperimeter is exactly half that count.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: 6-8-10 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=6+8+10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=24\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{24}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=12\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Doubling all three sides doubles both measurements: 24 is twice 12, and 12 is twice 6. Scaling the triangle scales its boundary measurements by the same factor.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=5+12+13]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=30\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{30}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=15\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Note that 15 exceeds the hypotenuse 13, as the triangle inequality check requires.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: 8-15-17 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=17]]')} />. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=8+15+17]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=40\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{40}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=20\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Again the semiperimeter clears the longest side: 20 against 17.</p>

