<!-- Section 10: Missing side -->
<section id="sec-missing-side" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Perimeter When One Side Is Missing</h2>
  <p>The calculator on this page requires all three side lengths. If one side is unknown, calculate it first with the Pythagorean theorem. The perimeter cannot be completed from two sides: two lengths describe an open corner, not a closed boundary.</p>

  <h3>Finding the Hypotenuse When Both Legs Are Known</h3>
  <p>Use the Pythagorean theorem in its hypotenuse form: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}]]]')} />. Example: <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{6^2+8^2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{36+64}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{100}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=10}]]]')} />. Now the perimeter can be calculated: <span set:html={rich('[[P=6+8+10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=24}]]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]]')} /> following from the halving.</p>

  <h3>Finding a Missing Leg When the Hypotenuse Is Known</h3>
  <p>Rearrange <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> to isolate the unknown leg: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=\\sqrt{c^2-b^2}}]]]')} />. Example: <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{13^2-12^2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{169-144}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{25}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=5}]]]')} />. The perimeter follows: <span set:html={rich('[[P=5+12+13=30]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=15]]')} />. The subtraction inside the root is the signature of a missing leg; a missing hypotenuse always adds.</p>

  <h3>Why Must the Missing Side Be Calculated First?</h3>
  <p>The perimeter requires all three boundary lengths. A missing side cannot simply be ignored or estimated without justification: two sides do not bound a triangle, and a guessed third side makes the total fiction. If only two sides are known, the <a href="/missing-leg-from-c-and-a-calculator/" class="text-primary underline">Right Triangle Side Calculator</a> finds the third before this page&apos;s calculator takes over. Note that this is an educational method, not an additional input mode in the existing calculator: the perimeter and semiperimeter modes still take three sides each.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('pythagoras-missing-side.svg')} alt="Right triangle with legs 6 and 8, unknown hypotenuse solved as 10, giving perimeter 24" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Legs 6 and 8 give c = 10, so P = 24 and s = 12.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Pythagorean theorem -->
<section id="sec-pythagoras" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Is the Pythagorean Theorem Related to Right Triangle Perimeter?</h2>
  <p>The theorem states <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a^2+b^2=c^2}]]]')} />. It establishes the relationship between the two legs and the hypotenuse: the square corner forces the three lengths into this exact balance. For perimeter work, the theorem is the bridge between an incomplete set of measurements and the complete boundary.</p>

  <h3>Why Does It Matter for Perimeter?</h3>
  <p>If the two legs are known, the hypotenuse can be calculated before finding the complete perimeter. Without the theorem, a missing side would block the calculation entirely; with it, two known sides are enough to recover the third and finish the sum. The theorem also runs the other direction: a known hypotenuse and one known leg recover the other leg. Either way, it turns a two-side problem into a three-side perimeter.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />. Find <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{5^2+12^2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{25+144}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{169}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=13}]]]')} />. Calculate the perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=5+12+13]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=30}]]]')} />. Calculate the semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{30}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=15}]]]')} />. One theorem, one addition, one halving: the complete chain from two legs to both boundary measurements.</p>

  <h3>How Can You Verify a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>The three measurements must satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />, with the hypotenuse as the longest side. Also check the triangle inequality: for any valid triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a+c>b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[b+c>a]]')} />. These conditions matter when checking measurements: a failed Pythagorean check means the triangle is not right-angled, or a side is mislabelled; a failed triangle inequality means the three lengths cannot form any triangle at all. Run both checks before trusting a perimeter built from field measurements.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Advanced uses of semiperimeter -->
<section id="sec-advanced" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Is Semiperimeter Used in Other Triangle Formulas?</h2>
  <p>This section gives the semiperimeter its mathematical depth without turning the page into a different calculator&apos;s tutorial. The semiperimeter is more than half a perimeter: it is the quantity that connects side lengths to area and circle geometry, and the formulas below are where it earns its keep.</p>

  <h3>Relationship Between Semiperimeter and Inradius</h3>
  <p>The area of any nondegenerate triangle equals its inradius times its semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=rs}]]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> is the area, <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> the inradius, and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> the semiperimeter. This relationship works for all nondegenerate triangles, not only right ones. For a right triangle, the inradius can also be calculated from the sides using <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}}]]]')} />. Take the 3-4-5 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{3+4-5}{2}=1]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6}]]]')} />. The semiperimeter is an important intermediate measurement in this calculation: it carries the boundary information while the inradius carries the circle information.</p>

  <h3>Relationship With Heron&apos;s Formula</h3>
  <p>Heron&apos;s formula computes the area from the three sides and the semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}}]]]')} />. It uses all three side lengths, it uses the semiperimeter, it calculates triangle area, and it works for any valid triangle. Use the 3-4-5 triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{6(6-3)(6-4)(6-5)}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{6\\times3\\times2\\times1}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{36}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6}]]]')} />. The semiperimeter appears four times inside the root, which shows how central it is: Heron&apos;s formula cannot even be written without it.</p>

  <h3>Why Is Semiperimeter a Foundation Measurement?</h3>
  <p>It is more than half a perimeter. It is a useful mathematical quantity that connects side lengths to area and circle geometry: the inradius relation treats it as the natural partner of <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, and Heron&apos;s formula builds the entire area on it. Whenever a formula needs the boundary distilled to a single convenient number, <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is that number. This page does not turn into a full Heron&apos;s formula or inradius tutorial; it notes the connection so the semiperimeter mode&apos;s output has somewhere to go next.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Perimeter vs area -->
<section id="sec-perimeter-area" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Difference Between Perimeter and Area?</h2>
  <p>One of the most persistent confusions in geometry is mixing up the outline with the surface. Perimeter and area share the same triangle but measure different things, in different units, answering different questions.</p>

  <h3>Perimeter Measures the Boundary</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} />. It measures the total length around the triangle: the edging, the trim, the fence line. Units are linear: metres, centimetres, feet, inches. It is one-dimensional, a length and nothing else.</p>

  <h3>Area Measures the Enclosed Surface</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ab}{2}}]]]')} />. It measures the flat two-dimensional region inside the right triangle: the glass in the window, the turf in the garden section. Units are square: square metres, square centimetres, square feet, square inches. It is two-dimensional, a surface count and nothing else.</p>

  <h3>Can Two Triangles Have the Same Perimeter but Different Areas?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Triangle 1: <span set:html={rich('[[a=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[P=5+12+13=30]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{5\\times12}{2}=30]]')} />. Triangle 2: <span set:html={rich('[[a=7.5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=12.5]]')} />. Check the right angle: <span set:html={rich('[[7.5^2+10^2=56.25+100=156.25=12.5^2]]')} />, so it is a genuine right triangle. Its perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[P=7.5+10+12.5=30]]')} />, the same 30, but its area is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{7.5\\times10}{2}=37.5]]')} />, clearly different. Equal perimeter does not automatically mean equal area: the outline length fixes the total edging, but the shape of the triangle decides how much surface that edging encloses.</p>

  <h3>Why Should You Never Report Perimeter in Square Units?</h3>
  <p>A perimeter is a one-dimensional length; square units describe a two-dimensional surface. Reporting 12 m² for a perimeter claims a surface where only an outline was measured, and anyone reading the number will picture the wrong thing. Keep the units honest: lengths in m, cm, ft, in; areas in m², cm², ft², in². The unit is part of the answer, and the wrong unit turns a right answer into a wrong one.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: Units and decimals -->
<section id="sec-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Measurement Units in Perimeter and Semiperimeter Calculations</h2>
  <p>Metres, centimetres, millimetres, kilometres, feet, inches, yards, miles: length units come in many sizes, and the perimeter formula accepts any of them. The one rule is agreement: all three sides must speak the same unit before they are added.</p>

  <h3>Why Must All Three Side Lengths Use Compatible Units?</h3>
  <p>The perimeter is a sum of lengths. Adding 3 feet to 48 inches without converting mixes two scales, and the total has no clean meaning: 56 of what? Before adding measurements, convert them into a common unit. The calculator&apos;s unit selectors exist for exactly this step, and the worked example below shows the conversion doing its quiet, essential work.</p>

  <h3>Worked Unit Conversion Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=48\\text{ inches}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ ft}]]')} />. Convert the middle side: <span set:html={rich('[[48\\text{ inches}=4\\text{ ft}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=12\\text{ ft}}]]]')} />. Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6\\text{ ft}}]]]')} />. Note that the converted triple is the familiar 3-4-5, which confirms the conversion independently: had the inches been added raw, the &quot;perimeter&quot; would have been 56 of nothing coherent.</p>

  <h3>Calculating With Decimal Side Measurements</h3>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a=2.5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=6.5]]')} />. First confirm the right angle: <span set:html={rich('[[2.5^2+6^2=6.25+36=42.25=6.5^2]]')} />. Calculate the perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=2.5+6+6.5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=15\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Then the semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{15}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=7.5\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Decimals flow through the same addition and halving as whole numbers; the formulas never require integers, and workshop measurements rarely are.</p>

  <h3>Why Should You Avoid Rounding Too Early?</h3>
  <p>Measurement calculations may contain decimal values, and every intermediate rounding injects error that the later steps cannot remove. Keep sufficient precision in the intermediate steps and round only the final answer according to the measurement accuracy required. Rounding 2.5 to 3 mid-calculation would corrupt both <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />; the calculator keeps full precision until the end, and hand calculations should do the same.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: Scaling -->
<section id="sec-scaling" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Do Changes in Side Lengths Affect Perimeter and Semiperimeter?</h2>
  <p>The perimeter is a linear measurement, so it scales linearly with the triangle: stretch every side by some factor and the boundary stretches by exactly that factor. The semiperimeter follows along, since it is always half the perimeter.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When Every Side Doubles?</h3>
  <p>Start from <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} />. If every side doubles, the new perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[P_2=2a+2b+2c]]')} />, which factors to <span set:html={rich('[[P_2=2(a+b+c)]]')} />. Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P_2=2P}]]]')} />. The semiperimeter also doubles, since halving a doubled total still doubles the half. The 3-4-5 to 6-8-10 pair shows it in numbers: 12 becomes 24, and 6 becomes 12.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When Every Side Is Tripled?</h3>
  <p>The same algebra with a factor of three gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P_2=3P}]]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s_2=3s}]]]')} />. The linear scaling relationship holds for any scale factor <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />: multiply every side by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> and both boundary measurements multiply by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />. This is why scale drawings are so well behaved: measure the drawing, multiply by the scale factor, and the real perimeter is done.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When Only One Side Changes?</h3>
  <p>Increasing one side while keeping the other two fixed changes the perimeter by the same amount: add 2 units to one side and <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> grows by exactly 2, because the perimeter is a plain sum. But in a right triangle the side lengths are tied together by the Pythagorean theorem, so arbitrary side changes may not preserve the right-angle condition. Lengthening a leg without adjusting the hypotenuse breaks <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />, and the shape is no longer right-angled: the perimeter formula still adds three numbers, but they no longer describe a right triangle.</p>

  <h3>Comparing Two Right Triangles</h3>
  <p>Put 3-4-5 beside 6-8-10. Side lengths: each side doubled, scale factor 2. Perimeter: 12 against 24. Semiperimeter: 6 against 12. Doubling all side lengths doubles both boundary measurements, exactly as the algebra predicts. The comparison also runs the other way: if two triangles&apos; perimeters stand in a 2:1 ratio and both are right-angled, their sides stand in that ratio too.</p>
</section>

