<!-- Section 17: Common mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Right Triangle Perimeter and Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>Nearly every wrong answer in this topic traces to a skipped side or a confused label rather than bad arithmetic. The sixteen mistakes below are the ones that appear again and again, each with its cause and its fix.</p>

  <h3>1. Forgetting to Include the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Using <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b]]')} /> on the 3-4-5 triangle gives 7, not 12. The mistake happens because the hypotenuse never touches the right angle, so it feels separate from the triangle proper. Fix: the boundary has three edges; count all three, <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>2. Confusing the Perimeter With the Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>Entering 6 where 12 belongs, or the reverse, halves or doubles the material estimate. The mistake comes from the two values sharing the same three sides. Fix: label every result <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> or <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />, and check <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{P}{2}]]')} /> before moving on.</p>

  <h3>3. Dividing the Perimeter by Two When Calculating Perimeter</h3>
  <p>Computing <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{3+4+5}{2}=6]]')} /> and calling it the perimeter understates the boundary by half. The mistake is running the semiperimeter formula out of habit. Fix: <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} /> has no division; the halving belongs to <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> only.</p>

  <h3>4. Forgetting to Divide by Two When Calculating Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>Reporting <span set:html={rich('[[s=12]]')} /> for the 3-4-5 triangle feeds Heron&apos;s formula a doubled <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> and corrupts the area. Fix: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} /> always ends with the halving; without it, the number is the perimeter.</p>

  <h3>5. Using the Wrong Side as the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Treating a leg as <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, for example entering a=5, b=4, c=3, scrambles every downstream formula. The mistake usually comes from reading the sides in the wrong order. Fix: <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the longest side, opposite the right angle; sort the three numbers and assign <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> to the largest.</p>

  <h3>6. Entering Measurements That Do Not Form a Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Sides 2, 3, 4 give <span set:html={rich('[[P=9]]')} /> arithmetically, but <span set:html={rich('[[2^2+3^2=13\\neq16]]')} />, so no right angle exists and the page&apos;s formulas rest on a false premise. Fix: verify <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> before calculating.</p>

  <h3>7. Mixing Centimetres and Metres</h3>
  <p>Adding 3 m, 40 cm, and 5 m as <span set:html={rich('[[3+40+5=48]]')} /> produces 48 of nothing. Fix: convert to one unit first: <span set:html={rich('[[40\\text{ cm}=0.4\\text{ m}]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[P=8.4\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>8. Mixing Feet and Inches</h3>
  <p>Adding 3 ft, 48 in, and 5 ft as 56 repeats the same scale error in imperial units. Fix: <span set:html={rich('[[48\\text{ in}=4\\text{ ft}]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[P=12\\text{ ft}]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>9. Reporting Perimeter in Square Units</h3>
  <p>Writing <span set:html={rich('[[P=12\\text{ m}^2]]')} />. The mistake usually comes from copying an area unit out of habit after working area problems. Fix: perimeters are lengths, so <span set:html={rich('[[P=12\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>10. Reporting Semiperimeter as an Area</h3>
  <p>Treating <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} /> as 6 square units. The semiperimeter is a length, half a boundary, not a surface. Fix: <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} /> units of length, full stop.</p>

  <h3>11. Rounding Too Early</h3>
  <p>Rounding 2.5 to 3 before adding gives <span set:html={rich('[[3+6+6.5=15.5]]')} /> instead of the true 15. The mistake feels harmless because the rounding looks small. Fix: keep full precision through the calculation and round only the final answer.</p>

  <h3>12. Using Only Two Sides in the Existing Calculator</h3>
  <p>Entering <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} /> and no <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> leaves the boundary open. The calculator needs all three lengths because a perimeter is a closed total. Fix: find the missing side first with the Pythagorean theorem, then calculate.</p>

  <h3>13. Ignoring the Triangle Inequality</h3>
  <p>Sides 2, 3, 10 give <span set:html={rich('[[P=15]]')} /> arithmetically, but <span set:html={rich('[[2+3<10]]')} />, so no triangle exists at all. Fix: check <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]]')} /> before trusting any result built from the three numbers.</p>

  <h3>14. Assuming the Semiperimeter Is a Physical Half-Boundary Path</h3>
  <p>Looking for the &quot;6-unit walk&quot; along the 3-4-5 edges. No single continuous half-path equals <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> in general; <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is half the numerical total. Fix: treat <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> as a number for formulas, not a route to walk.</p>

  <h3>15. Confusing Perimeter With the Sum of Only the Perpendicular Legs</h3>
  <p>Computing <span set:html={rich('[[a+b=7]]')} /> and stopping, as if the hypotenuse were interior decoration. Fix: the hypotenuse is a boundary edge; <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} />, all three terms, every time.</p>

  <h3>16. Assuming Perimeter Alone Determines the Triangle&apos;s Area</h3>
  <p>Believing <span set:html={rich('[[P=30]]')} /> fixes <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} />. The 5-12-13 triangle (<span set:html={rich('[[K=30]]')} />) and the 7.5-10-12.5 triangle (<span set:html={rich('[[K=37.5]]')} />) share <span set:html={rich('[[P=30]]')} /> with different areas. Fix: area needs more than the boundary total; use the legs or Heron&apos;s formula.</p>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct: A Concrete Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />. Correct perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5=12]]')} />. Incorrectly omitting the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[3+4=7]]')} />. Seven is not the complete perimeter because the boundary&apos;s third edge was never counted. Correct semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{12}{2}=6]]')} />. Six is not the full boundary length: it is half of it, the value formulas like Heron&apos;s need. The arithmetic looks confident in all three cases; only the geometry tells them apart.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Right Triangle Perimeter Is Correct</h2>
  <p>Seven independent checks, all demonstrated on the 3-4-5 triangle (<span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[P=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />). Agreement between different routes is the strongest evidence an answer is right, in homework and in practical measurement alike.</p>

  <h3>Method 1: Add All Three Sides Again</h3>
  <p>Run <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} /> fresh: <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5=12]]')} />. A second pass with the numbers re-entered catches typos and mis-keyed digits that a single pass hides.</p>

  <h3>Method 2: Divide the Perimeter by Two</h3>
  <p>Compute <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{P}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{12}{2}=6]]')} />. If the halving does not match the semiperimeter mode&apos;s output, one of the two calculations went wrong and both deserve a second look.</p>

  <h3>Method 3: Double the Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>Check <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=2s}]]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[P=2\\times6=12]]')} />. This is the reverse of Method 2 and catches the same class of error from the other direction: a wrong <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> fails here even when the original addition looked fine.</p>

  <h3>Method 4: Verify the Pythagorean Relationship</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=9+16=25=5^2]]')} />. This checks the side identification behind every other calculation; a wrong hypotenuse means a side was mislabeled, and <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> both need rebuilding.</p>

  <h3>Method 5: Check the Triangle Inequality</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[3+4=7>5]]')} />. The three lengths must be able to form a triangle at all before the right-angle check means anything. A failure here sends you back to the measurements, not to the arithmetic.</p>

  <h3>Method 6: Check the Measurement Units</h3>
  <p>Confirm that all three sides use compatible units. Mixed units are the quietest error in this topic: the arithmetic looks clean while the scale is wrong. Re-read each input&apos;s unit selector before trusting the total.</p>

  <h3>Method 7: Compare With the Live Visualization</h3>
  <p>The calculator draws the triangle from the entered sides. If the drawing looks nothing like the measured object, a side was misassigned: the longest entered side should sit opposite the square corner. The picture is an independent witness, and it is worth a glance before the numbers go into an order or a report.</p>
</section>

