<!-- Conclusion -->
<section id="sec-conclusion" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>One Boundary, Two Useful Measurements</h2>
  <p>The perimeter totals the boundary; the semiperimeter halves that total for the formulas that need it. The 3-4-5 triangle carries the whole story in miniature: <span set:html={rich('[[P=12]]')} /> units of outline for the material order, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} /> units ready for Heron&apos;s formula or the inradius relation. One addition and one halving cover both jobs.</p>
  <p>The working habit is simple: label the hypotenuse correctly, include all three sides, match the units, keep decimals unrounded until the end, and verify through a second method. With those habits, <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} /> are two of the most dependable measurements in geometry.</p>
</section>
